---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Snow Blowers",
    "url": "/best-snow-blower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Snow Shovels",
    "url": "/best-snow-shovel/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Cordless Snow Shovels",
    "url": "/best-cordless-snow-shovel/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Roof Rakes",
    "url": "/best-roof-rake/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Ski Gloves",
    "url": "/best-ski-gloves/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Heated Driveway Mats",
    "url": "/best-heated-driveway-mat/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-27", modified: "2026-11-27" });
const title = "7 Best Heated Gloves in 2026: Warm Fingers, Honest Runtime";
const description =
  "Heated gloves rarely last as long as the box says, and many heat the back of your hand more than your fingers. Here are 7 picks that keep fingers warm for real work.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "ororo-premium-heated-gloves",
    "name": "ORORO Premium Heated Gloves",
    "category": "Insulated heated glove, leather palm",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "description": "This is the glove we would hand to most people who clear their own driveway and want one pair that does everything reasonably well. The listing states 150g of 3M Thinsulate insulation, a leather palm, touchscreen fingertips, a water- and wind-resistant shell and rechargeable batteries. The insulation figure is the spec that matters most here, and it is the one shoppers skip. A heated glove with thin insulation is cold the moment the battery dies, and it burns through charge faster because the heat leaks straight out. With 150g of fill, this glove still behaves like a proper winter glove on its lowest setting or switched off, which is how you stretch a battery across a long morning of shoveling and salting. The leather palm grips a shovel shaft or a snow blower handle better than a slick synthetic face and wears longer. The trade-off is bulk: this is a full winter glove, so fine work like threading a pull cord or handling small screws is clumsy, and it is not fully waterproof, so soaking-wet slush will get through in time. Owners commonly report that the heat favors the back of the hand and the fingers rather than the palm, which is normal for the category. If you mainly need dexterity, buy a heated liner instead; if you spend hours in wet snow, the Fieldsheer below has a waterproof liner.",
    "pros": [
      "150g 3M Thinsulate keeps hands warm even with the heat off",
      "Leather palm grips shovels and handles and resists wear",
      "Water- and wind-resistant shell suits dry snow and wind",
      "Touchscreen fingertips for quick phone use",
      "Rechargeable batteries included in the listing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Bulky for fine work like pull cords or small fasteners",
      "Water-resistant, not waterproof, in heavy slush",
      "Palm gets little direct heat, like most heated gloves"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best all-round heated glove for snow clearing and cold errands: real insulation first, heat second.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "fieldsheer-squall-2-heated-glove",
    "name": "Fieldsheer Squall 2.0 Heated Glove 7.4V",
    "category": "Waterproof heated glove",
    "badge": "Best for Wet Snow",
    "description": "Fieldsheer's Mobile Warming line has been making battery-heated apparel for years, and the Squall 2.0 is the one to look at if your winter is wet rather than bitter. The listing specifies a 7.4V system with four heat settings, a waterproof liner, 3M Thinsulate insulation, touchscreen compatibility and an 8-hour battery rating. The waterproof liner is the reason it ranks here. Heavy, wet snow and slush soak through a water-resistant glove in an hour of shoveling, and once the insulation is damp no heating element can keep up: water pulls heat away from skin far faster than air. A glove that stays dry inside lets the battery do its job. Four settings instead of the usual three also matter more than they sound, because the gap between a low and a medium setting is where most of your runtime is decided. Read the 8-hour figure as a low-setting number. On the top setting, expect a small fraction of that, which is true of every heated glove sold. The trade-offs are the usual ones for a waterproof glove: a membrane makes it a little warmer and a little less breathable, so hands can get clammy during hard shoveling, and the fit is bulkier than a liner. If you work in dry cold and want leather durability, the ORORO or Savior Heat work glove is a better fit.",
    "pros": [
      "Waterproof liner keeps insulation dry in slush and wet snow",
      "Four heat settings give finer control over runtime",
      "3M Thinsulate insulation backs up the heating",
      "Rated for up to 8 hours on its lowest setting",
      "Touchscreen compatible fingertips"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Membrane can feel clammy during heavy shoveling",
      "High-setting runtime is far shorter than the headline figure",
      "Bulkier than a liner for detailed tasks"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Pick this one if your snow is heavy and wet: a dry glove is a warm glove, and this one is built to stay dry.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "klein-tools-60795-heated-liners",
    "name": "Klein Tools 60795 Heated Work Glove Liners and Knit Dipped Gloves",
    "category": "Heated liner and work glove set",
    "badge": "Best for Job Sites",
    "description": "Klein Tools is a name most electricians and tradespeople already trust, and this set is the closest thing we found to a heated glove designed around actual work. The listing describes rechargeable heated liners paired with knit dipped outer gloves, cut level A1 protection, touchscreen compatibility and batteries included. The size listed is a large liner with a 2XL outer, which tells you how it is meant to be worn: a thin heated layer against the skin and a cheap, replaceable grippy glove over it. That two-layer setup is the reason it ranks for job sites. Dipped work gloves wear out fast on rebar, lumber and pallets, and replacing a dipped shell costs far less than replacing a heated glove. The liner keeps its heat close to the fingers, and you keep enough dexterity to drive screws, handle fasteners or work a tape. The A1 cut rating is the entry level of the ANSI scale, so treat it as protection against light nicks, not a glove for sheet metal or glass. Knit dipped gloves are also not waterproof or heavily insulated, so in deep cold or wet snow you will want a warmer outer shell over the liners. If your work is mostly snow clearing rather than building, the ORORO Premium or Fieldsheer is the warmer single glove.",
    "pros": [
      "Heated liners plus replaceable knit dipped outer gloves",
      "Cut level A1 rated per the listing",
      "Thin liner keeps fine dexterity for tools and fasteners",
      "Touchscreen compatible, batteries included",
      "Trusted trade brand with a work-first design"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A1 is the lowest cut level, light protection only",
      "Knit outer gloves are not waterproof or warm in deep cold",
      "Sizing pairs a large liner with a 2XL shell, so check fit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The smartest heated setup for trades: heat in a thin liner, wear in a cheap outer glove you can replace.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "savior-heat-leather-heated-work-gloves",
    "name": "SAVIOR HEAT Heated Work Gloves, Goat Leather",
    "category": "Leather heated work glove",
    "badge": "Best for Yard Work",
    "description": "Savior Heat is one of the most established names in battery-heated gloves on Amazon, and this goat leather work glove is its answer to people who use their hands hard in winter. The listing states premium goat leather, its EH heating system, 180g of 3M Thinsulate, a 13-hour runtime figure, USB-C fast charging and touchscreen fingertips. Goat leather is the spec to care about. It is supple, so the glove bends with your hand when you grip a wheelbarrow, split kindling or haul firewood, and it holds up to abrasion that shreds synthetic shells. The 180g insulation is heavier than most heated gloves list, which means it stays warm with the heat on low and still has something to give when the battery is flat. USB-C charging is a small but real convenience: you can top up from a phone charger or a power bank in the truck rather than hunting for a proprietary cable. Treat the 13-hour figure as the lowest setting under ideal conditions; on high, in real cold, expect a few hours. Leather also needs care. It stiffens if it dries next to a heater, and unless treated it will take on water in slush. If you want maximum dexterity, a heated liner will beat any leather glove; if your snow is wet, the Fieldsheer's waterproof liner is the safer bet.",
    "pros": [
      "Goat leather is flexible and resists abrasion",
      "180g 3M Thinsulate is heavier than most heated gloves list",
      "USB-C charging from common chargers and power banks",
      "Touchscreen compatible fingertips",
      "Listed runtime of up to 13 hours on low"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Leather needs conditioning and slow drying",
      "Not a waterproof glove for long sessions in slush",
      "13-hour figure is a best case, not a high-heat number"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for firewood, yard cleanup and hard-wearing outdoor chores where a synthetic glove would shred.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "ororo-heated-glove-liners",
    "name": "ORORO Heated Glove Liners",
    "category": "Heated glove liner",
    "badge": "Best Heated Liner",
    "description": "If the reason you want heated gloves is that your fingers go numb and stay numb, a liner is often the better buy than a full glove, and this is the liner we would start with. ORORO's liner listings describe a lightweight stretch-fit glove built for layering, with heat across the fingers and the back of the hand, touchscreen fingertips and a non-slip palm. The point of a liner is that the heating sits right against your skin, with nothing between the element and your fingers. A full heated glove has to push heat through a lining and a gap; a liner does not. You then choose the outer layer for the job: a leather work glove for hauling, a waterproof shell for slush, an oversized mitten for the coldest mornings, or nothing at all for a quick walk to the mailbox. You also keep far more dexterity than any insulated heated glove allows, which matters for keys, zips, phone screens and small tools. The catch is that a liner on its own is thin. Worn alone in real cold, it loses heat quickly and drains its battery faster trying to keep up, so plan to wear something over it outdoors. Liners also take more wear at the fingertips. If you only want one glove that does everything, the ORORO Premium is simpler.",
    "pros": [
      "Heat sits directly against the fingers and back of the hand",
      "Thin stretch fit keeps dexterity for keys, zips and tools",
      "Layers under work gloves, shells or mittens",
      "Touchscreen fingertips and non-slip palm",
      "Easy to move between outdoor jobs and driving"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Too thin to wear alone in real cold",
      "Battery drains faster without an insulating outer layer",
      "Fingertips wear faster than a full glove"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best choice for cold fingers and fine work: heat against the skin, any outer glove you like on top.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "volt-heated-leather-work-gloves",
    "name": "Volt Battery Heated Leather Work Gloves",
    "category": "Leather heated work glove",
    "badge": "Heavy-Duty Pick",
    "description": "Volt Resistance is a smaller heated apparel brand, and its leather work glove is one of the few heated gloves on Amazon that is plainly built as a work glove first. The listing describes a rechargeable battery heated leather work glove for men, aimed at work and hunting. That framing matters because most heated gloves are ski or motorcycle gloves with a work label added. A real work glove has a cut that lets you close your hand around a handle without the glove bunching in the palm, and leather that can take shovels, chains, firewood and fencing. If you spend long days outside on a farm, a site or a big property, that durability is worth more than an extra heat setting. The trade-offs are clear. It sits near the top of the price range for battery-heated gloves, it is listed in men's sizes, and the listing does not give as much detail on insulation weight or runtime as Savior Heat or Fieldsheer do, so you are paying partly for the brand's reputation among outdoor workers. Leather also needs care to stay soft and shed water. If you want the most features for the money, the Savior Heat leather glove lists more. If you want a glove cut like a work glove, this is the one to look at.",
    "pros": [
      "Built as a leather work glove first, not a ski glove",
      "Leather stands up to firewood, chains and tools",
      "Rechargeable battery heating",
      "Suits long outdoor workdays and hunting",
      "Offered in a wide run of sizes, small to 2XL"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Near the top of the heated glove price range",
      "Listing gives fewer details on insulation and runtime",
      "Men’s sizing only per the listing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A tough leather work glove with heat added, for people who wear out ordinary heated gloves in a season.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  },
  {
    "id": "outdoor-research-prevail-heated-mitt",
    "name": "Outdoor Research Prevail Heated GORE-TEX Mitt",
    "category": "Heated mitten, GORE-TEX shell",
    "badge": "Warmest for Cold Fingers",
    "description": "Mittens are warmer than gloves, and that physics does not change when you add a battery. Four fingers sharing one pocket lose far less heat than four fingers each wrapped in their own tube, and a heating element in a mitten warms that shared air pocket rather than trying to chase heat down each finger. That is why the warmest pick on this list is a mitten. The Outdoor Research Prevail pairs a GORE-TEX shell with heating, from a brand known for serious cold-weather gear. GORE-TEX is the detail that justifies the price: a proven waterproof, breathable membrane keeps the insulation dry from outside, which is what lets heat do its job hour after hour. For anyone whose fingers go white or numb in the cold and who mostly needs to stay warm rather than handle small parts, this is the style to look at. The cost is steep: this sits at the premium end, well above every other pick here. You also give up dexterity. You cannot work a zip, a key or a small fastener in a mitten, so most people pair one with a thin liner and slip the mitten off for fiddly jobs. If price matters more than peak warmth, a heated liner under an ordinary insulated mitten is the budget version of the same idea.",
    "pros": [
      "Mitten design is inherently warmer than separate fingers",
      "GORE-TEX shell keeps insulation dry in snow",
      "From a well-known cold-weather gear brand",
      "Heat warms the shared finger pocket where it counts",
      "Best fit for hands that go numb before anything else does"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium price, the most expensive pick here",
      "Poor dexterity for zips, keys and small parts",
      "Listing offers limited runtime detail"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For the coldest fingers, a heated mitten beats any heated glove; this is the premium version, built to stay dry.",
    "amazonUrl": "#"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Heated Gloves",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-heated-gloves/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the best heated gloves?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, the ORORO Premium Heated Gloves are the best all-round choice: the listing states 150g of 3M Thinsulate, a leather palm and a water- and wind-resistant shell, so they stay warm even on low. For wet snow, choose the Fieldsheer Squall 2.0. For job sites, the Klein Tools heated liner set is smarter."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do heated gloves really last on a charge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect the box figure only on the lowest setting. Listings commonly quote 6 to 13 hours, but on high most heated gloves give a few hours, and cold batteries deliver less. Plan around two to three hours of strong heat, use low as your default, keep batteries warm until you head out, and carry spares for long days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are heated gloves worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They are worth it if you spend long stretches outside with your hands still, such as running a snow blower, working on a site or watching outdoor events, or if your fingers get cold long before the rest of you. For short, active jobs like quick shoveling, a well-insulated ordinary glove is often enough."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are heated glove liners better than heated gloves?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Liners are better for dexterity and for cold fingers, because the heat sits directly on your skin and you can choose the outer glove. Full heated gloves are simpler and warmer on their own. Many people use liners under an oversized insulated glove or mitten, which is the warmest combination of all."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do heated gloves help with Raynaud's?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Heated gloves are not a medical treatment and we make no medical claims. Many people with cold-sensitive fingers find them more comfortable, especially designs that heat the fingertips. If your fingers change color or lose feeling in the cold, talk to a doctor, and use low settings since reduced sensation can hide a burn."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use my Milwaukee or DeWalt tool battery with heated gloves?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "When we searched Amazon, we did not find heated gloves from Milwaukee Tool, DeWalt or Ryobi that run on their tool batteries. Heated gloves sold as Milwaukee Leather are from a separate company and use their own packs. Tool-battery heated jackets and vests are common, and keeping your core warm helps your hands too."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you wash heated gloves?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most heated gloves are hand-wash only, and you must remove the batteries first. Use mild soap and cool water, avoid soaking the cuff connector, and let them air dry away from direct heat, which stiffens leather. Never tumble dry or machine wash a heated glove unless the maker's care label specifically allows it."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best heated gloves",
      "heated gloves",
      "heated glove liners",
      "heated work gloves",
      "battery heated gloves",
      "heated gloves for raynauds"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          7 Best Heated Gloves in 2026: Warm Fingers, Honest Runtime
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          The runtime on a heated glove box is measured on the lowest setting, and most heat goes to the back of your hand. Here is what actually keeps fingers warm: finger heat, real insulation, a spare battery and the right glove for the job.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ororo-premium-heated-gloves"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See All 7 Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#battery-life" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Battery Life: The Real Numbers</a>
          <a href="#heat-coverage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Where Heated Gloves Heat</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 7 Best Heated Gloves</a>
          <a href="#gloves-vs-liners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Gloves vs Liners</a>
          <a href="#snow-clearing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. For Snow Clearing</a>
          <a href="#work-gloves" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. For Job Site and Yard Work</a>
          <a href="#cold-fingers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. For Raynaud's-Type Cold Hands</a>
          <a href="#battery-care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Make Batteries Last</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Are Heated Gloves Safe?</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. How to Size Heated Gloves</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Heated gloves look like the answer to cold hands, and they can be, but two things trip buyers up. The big runtime number on the box is a low-setting figure, so on high you get a few hours, not a full day. And most gloves heat the back of the hand more than the fingertips. We picked gloves, liners and a mitten for snow clearing, yard and job-site work, and hands that get cold first, and we explain what each one really does.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="battery-life" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Heated Glove Battery Life: Expect a Fraction of the Box Number
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every heated glove listing leads with a big runtime figure: 8 hours, 11 hours, 13 hours. Those figures are real, but they describe the lowest heat setting, usually measured in mild conditions with a fresh battery. On the top setting the same gloves last a small fraction of that. Savior Heat's own liner listing, for example, gives a range of 2 to 9 hours across its settings, and that spread is typical of the category. If you buy expecting a full day of high heat, you will be disappointed by lunchtime.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Heated Glove Runtime Is Rated</h3>

          <p>
            Runtime is set by battery capacity and the power the heating element draws. Most heated gloves use a pair of 7.4V packs, one per glove, of roughly 2,000 to 3,000 mAh, though some newer listings quote higher capacities. A small battery driving a heating element on full power empties quickly; the same battery on low lasts several times longer. Manufacturers quote the low number because it is the biggest. There is nothing dishonest about it, but it is not the number you will live with on a hard January morning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold Batteries Give Less</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries deliver less of their stored energy when they are cold, and a heated glove keeps its battery in a cuff pocket right where the wind hits it. Charge batteries indoors, keep them at room temperature until you head out, and do not leave spare packs in a freezing truck. A warm battery inside a jacket pocket will give you noticeably more of its rated capacity than one that has sat on a cold workbench.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Rule for Planning Your Day</h3>

          <p>
            Plan on two to three hours of genuinely useful heat per charge if you run the gloves on high or medium, and use the low setting as your default. Start on high for a few minutes to warm your hands, then drop down. If you work outside for longer than a morning, buy a glove sold with a second battery set or order spares from the same brand. A cheap spare battery set is the single best upgrade to any heated glove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Heated Gloves Actually Heat: Back of the Hand, Fingers, Rarely the Palm
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second thing listings blur is where the heat goes. Most heated gloves put a heating panel across the back of the hand and run thin wires along the backs of the fingers. A few wrap the wires around the fingertips. Very few heat the palm, because a heating element there would be crushed against every handle you grip and would wear through quickly. For most people that layout is fine. For anyone whose fingertips are the problem, it matters a great deal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back-of-Hand Panels</h3>

          <p>
            A warm panel on the back of the hand warms the blood flowing toward the fingers, which helps the whole hand feel warmer. It is also the easiest place to put a heating element, so it is the part every heated glove does well. If a listing only mentions back-of-hand heat, assume the fingers get much less.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Finger Heat and Fingertip Wrap</h3>

          <p>
            Look for listings that describe heat running along each finger and, ideally, around the tips. Fingertips are where cold hands hurt first and where they recover last. Gloves and liners that state full-finger or fingertip heating are worth paying a little more for if your fingers go numb before the rest of you feels cold.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thumbs and Palms</h3>

          <p>
            Thumbs are often an afterthought. Some gloves heat them, many do not. Palms are almost never heated. That is one reason a mitten feels warmer than a glove with the same heating: your fingers press together and share warmth, and the palm side benefits from the air pocket instead of a cold handle.
          </p>

