          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Heated gloves work best when you treat the heat as a top-up, not the whole plan. Buy for insulation and a dry lining first, check that the heat reaches your fingers, and expect a few hours on high rather than the box figure.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For snow clearing, the ORORO Premium or Fieldsheer Squall 2.0 is the easy pick. For work, a heated liner under a replaceable glove or a leather heated work glove lasts longest. For the coldest fingers, a heated mitten or liner-and-mitten combination wins. Whatever you choose, a spare battery set is money well spent.
          </p>
